#X text 35 29 The drawing commands are:;
#X text 70 57 -line \; -rectangle \; -roundedrect (rounded rectangle) \; -ellipse \; -path (custom shapes) \; -arc \; -curve \; -quad;
#X text 30 166 You need to set a color and other parameters before setting shapes \, these stick around if unchanged for following shapes. The 'linewidth' message sets the width used by the stroke commands (unfilled shapes) that come after it., f 61;
#X text 41 634 Stroke commands also include: line \, curve \, quad \, .;
#X text 864 30 draw line;
#X msg 476 31 clear \, color 200 140 140 \, linewidth 4 \, line 20 20 180 180 \, bang, f 63;
#X text 30 223 All commands are followed by a start position coordinate pair (x/y). A line also needs an end coordinate point. For other shapes \, the concept is setting the width and heigth. There are rectangle \, roundrect and ellipse. These also take an optional argument to set "fill" (default) or "stroke" (for unfilled shapes)., f 61;
#X text 30 301 A 'path' command takes several (3 or more) points as coordinate pairs. It also takes an optional 'fill' or 'stroke' argument \, but defaults to stroke. to a 'arc' takes the same x/y/width/height box as 'ellipse' plus a start and end angle in degrees \, measured from the top and going clockwise. 'stroke' draws the arc itself and 'fill' fills the pie slice back to the centre., f 69;
#X msg 643 327 clear \, color 60 130 210 \, arc 20 20 160 160 -60 240 fill \, color 30 100 50 \, ellipse 110 60 10 10 \, bang, f 50;
#X msg 629 292 clear \, color 60 130 210 \, linewidth 10 \, arc 20 20 160 160 0 270 \, bang, f 38;
#X text 30 378 The 'arc' command takes the same x/y/width/height info (as 'ellipse' or 'rectangle') plus a start and end angle in degrees \, measured from the top and going clockwise. A 'stroke' optional argument (default) draws the arc itself \, while 'fill' does fill the pie slice back to the centre., f 69;
